load @term/seed/structure/set
  find set

load @term/seed/computation/spr
  find spr-cycle

# Content-addressable memory: memory accessed by what is stored, not where. You present a value (the comparand) and the
# memory returns the locations whose contents match, in parallel, in one step, instead of walking addresses. Associative
# computing extends this from a passive memory to a content-addressable ARRAY OF PROCESSORS: each matched location can
# also compute. The {3,4,3,4} bulk is exactly this, a content-addressable memory whose cells are docks of the mesh and
# whose matching is the query wave, with the Coxeter-word address of a cell available but rarely needed (you find a
# memory by its content, not its address). The form carries the SPR cycle the memory answers through.
form content-addressable-memory
  link cycle, like spr-cycle

# An association: several parallel fields grouped into one logical record per cell (one word), so a search that matches
# one field selects the whole record. In the bulk the association is the twenty-four-slot coin of a cell: a match on one
# direction-slot selects the whole coin. The form carries the set of fields that travel together.
form association
  link fields, like set

# A structure code: an address-free encoding of a linked structure (a list, a tree, a graph) directly in the table, so
# that pointer-following is replaced by parallel search over codes. Graph structure codes connect to the bulk's
# Coxeter-word addressing of the honeycomb: the tree of the splitting method is itself a structure code. The form
# carries the set of coded records.
form structure-code
  link records, like set
